00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.410
GARRY KASPAROV: Check.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.820
Chess is a game of
unlimited beauty.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.340
But it's not just
checks and attacks.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.210
You have to be creative.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.110
I'm here to share with you my
knowledge of the game of chess.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.990
But I'm trying to come up with
the comprehensive picture that

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.930
will be helpful for you
to become a better chess

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.190
player, and even a
better decision maker.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.090
Let's cover it.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.840
king g5, king c2, king f6, king
b3, king b7, king c4, king d6,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.980
king b5, king c7, king a6.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.230
Boom.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.060
White lost, yes?

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.800
Queen takes the knight at
5, and it's a double attack.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.150
Skewer and attraction,
and it's mate.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.660
What an amazing mate.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.210
How can we prevent this
rook from stopping our pawn?

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.019
Let's pause, stop
the video, and think.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:16.500
Okay.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.492
You have time to find
this winning combination.

00:01:20.492 --> 00:01:21.200
Did you see that?

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.150
Your bishop was hanging--

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:22.300
Man: There, yeah.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.425
--but then you have now c5.

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:25.105
 Ah, no, I didn't
see that line.

00:01:25.105 --> 00:01:25.980
 That's interesting.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.420
So you attack my bishop,
then you protect your bishop.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:28.920
 Right.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.100
 So let's go back and see
whether we can improve,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.670
and what's the
next logical moves?

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.080
It's about coming
up with new ideas,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.850
challenging your own excellence.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.110
Find your mistakes.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.760
Next time, you
have to be better.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.340
I've been thinking about a
few ideas that I never used.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.627
The one I want to show--

00:01:47.627 --> 00:01:48.585
it's another sacrifice.

00:01:48.585 --> 00:01:50.030
I like sacrifices.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.620
And it's one of my
favorite openings,

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.790
so just let's have a look.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.710
We cannot waste time.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.050
Time is precious.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.630
If you don't feel that
the game can unleash

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.310
your creative
potential, then you're

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.230
already on the losing side.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.760
It's not about
winning or losing.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.302
But of course, at
the end of the day,

00:02:06.302 --> 00:02:08.289
it's about winning or losing.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.900
I'm Garry Kasparov, and
this is my MasterClass.

